<mujoco model="g1_scene_ramp">
  <!--
    Flat ground with a gentle 3 degree ramp starting flush at the origin and
    rising along +x. For testing terrain generalisation: the stack has to notice
    that the ground is no longer at z = 0 and place the swing foot accordingly.

    slope.xml is not usable for this. It sits at x in [-5, -1] and, being a
    rotated box with its low corner in the air, presents a ~0.27 m ledge at its
    near edge - a step up, not a ramp. Here the slab is positioned so that the LOW
    edge of its top surface lands exactly on (x, z) = (1.5, 0), flush with the floor.

    It starts 1.5 m ahead of the spawn rather than at the origin so the robot has
    clear flat ground to spawn and settle on and then walks ONTO the ramp. With
    the low edge at the origin the robot was being thrown at spawn - angular rates
    of 8.6 rad/s, down at t = 0.3 s - because its feet straddle x = 0 and sit
    inside the slab edge before the controller has done anything.

    Geometry: half-size (2, 1, 0.02), rotated -3 deg about Y so the surface rises
    with +x (a positive rotation about +Y tilts local +x downward, hence the
    negative sign), centred at (3.49831, 0, 0.084699). That puts the top
    surface at z = 0 at x = 1.5 and z = 0.209 at x = 5.49.

    The slab is deliberately THIN. A thick box positioned to present the same
    surface buries most of its volume under the infinite floor plane, and MuJoCo
    resolves that as a huge static penetration - the robot was being thrown at
    spawn with angular rates of 8.6 rad/s, falling at t = 0.3 s, before it had
    taken a step. The contact exclusion below is kept as well, but keeping the
    penetration small is what actually makes the scene usable.
  -->

  <!-- legged robot -->
  <include file="g1_29dof.xml"/>

  <!-- MuJoCo simulation timestep. -->
  <option timestep="0.0005"/>

  <statistic center="1.0 0.7 1.0" extent="0.8"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.1 0.1 0.1" specular="0.9 0.9 0.9"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="-140" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb2="0.835 0.761 0.612" rgb1="0.639 0.722 0.8" width="512" height="512"/>

    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="random"
         rgb1="0.25 0.3 0.35" rgb2="0.15 0.2 0.25"
         markrgb="0.6 0.7 0.8"
         width="512" height="512"/>

    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="2 2" reflectance="0.05"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="1 0 3.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="groundplane"
          priority="1" friction="0.8 0.1 0.001"/>

    <body name="ramp" pos="3.49831 0 0.084699">
      <geom name="ramp_surface"
            type="box"
            size="2.0 1.0 0.02"
            quat="0.9996573 0 -0.0261769 0"
            rgba="0.55 0.6 0.65 1"
            priority="1"
            friction="0.8 0.1 0.001"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <contact>
    <!--
      The near end of the slab still dips a few centimetres below the floor plane.
      Skip that static pair so it cannot generate contact forces; both geoms still
      collide with the robot normally.
    -->
    <exclude body1="world" body2="ramp"/>
  </contact>

</mujoco>
